Chapter: GopDraw
  cites Kernel chapter BitmapFont

 Drawing primitives for the GOP framebuffer on the Option A boot path.
 The framebuffer is linear 32-bit XRGB; base and stride (pixels per
 scan line) come from the boot stub's handoff cells. Text renders from
 the CBF bitmap font: each glyph is 8x16, and every on-pixel is drawn
 as a scale-by-scale square, so the same font renders 8x16 at scale 1
 and 16x32 at scale 2. Panels 1024 pixels wide or wider use scale 2.

 The mouse cursor is drawn by saving the pixels beneath it, painting a
 small white block with a dark border, and restoring the patch on the
 next move -- a full-panel repaint per mouse twitch would run at
 report rate for nothing. Any draw that clears or repaints beneath the
 cursor invalidates the saved patch; the next movement repaints the
 cursor over the fresh screen. State block: [0] valid, [4] x, [8] y,
 [12] clamped width, [16] clamped height, saved pixels from [32] at a
 fixed 16-pixel pitch.

 We say:

Section: Cursor

  cursor-w : Integer = 8
  cursor-h : Integer = 12

  cursor-save-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-save-cols (cs) (base) (stride) (r) (c) =
    if c >= peek-32 cs 12 then 0
    else let a = poke-32 cs (32 + (r * 16 + c) * 4) (peek-32 base (((peek-32 cs 8 + r) * stride + peek-32 cs 4 + c) * 4))
    in cursor-save-cols cs base stride r (c + 1)

  cursor-save-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-save-rows (cs) (base) (stride) (r) =
    if r >= peek-32 cs 16 then 0
    else let cc = cursor-save-cols cs base stride r 0
    in cursor-save-rows cs base stride (r + 1)

  cursor-rest-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-rest-cols (cs) (base) (stride) (r) (c) =
    if c >= peek-32 cs 12 then 0
    else let a = poke-32 base (((peek-32 cs 8 + r) * stride + peek-32 cs 4 + c) * 4) (peek-32 cs (32 + (r * 16 + c) * 4))
    in cursor-rest-cols cs base stride r (c + 1)

  cursor-rest-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-rest-rows (cs) (base) (stride) (r) =
    if r >= peek-32 cs 16 then 0
    else let cc = cursor-rest-cols cs base stride r 0
    in cursor-rest-rows cs base stride (r + 1)

  cursor-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

 Hide before a repaint, not just clear the flag: clearing alone is
 correct only when the repaint covers the cursor. A pane that covers
 part of the panel leaves the old cursor pixels standing outside it,
 and the next save-under bakes them into the patch -- a permanent
 ghost the first restore stamps back. Restore first, then clear;
 the repaint may then paint anywhere, and the next update saves a
 clean patch.

  cursor-hide : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-hide (cs) (base) (stride) =
    if peek-32 cs 0 == 0 then 0
    else let r = cursor-rest-rows cs base stride 0
    in poke-32 cs 0 0

  cursor-update : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cursor-update (cs) (base) (stride) (pw) (ph) (s) (mx) (my) =
    let r0 = if peek-32 cs 0 /= 0 then cursor-rest-rows cs base stride 0 else 0
    in let cwc = cursor-min (cursor-w * s) (pw - mx)
    in let chc = cursor-min (cursor-h * s) (ph - my)
    in let s0 = poke-32 cs 4 mx
    in let s1 = poke-32 cs 8 my
    in let s2 = poke-32 cs 12 cwc
    in let s3 = poke-32 cs 16 chc
    in let sv = cursor-save-rows cs base stride 0
    in let d0 = gop-fill-rect base stride mx my cwc chc 1052688
    in let d1 = if cwc > 2 * s then (if chc > 2 * s then gop-fill-rect base stride (mx + s) (my + s) (cwc - 2 * s) (chc - 2 * s) 16777215 else 0) else 0
    in poke-32 cs 0 1

Section: Pixel

  gop-put : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-put (base) (stride) (x) (y) (color) =
    poke-32 base ((y * stride + x) * 4) color

Section: Rectangles

  gop-rect-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-rect-cols (base) (stride) (px) (py) (pw) (color) (x) =
    if x >= pw then 0
    else let p = gop-put base stride (px + x) py color
    in gop-rect-cols base stride px py pw color (x + 1)

  gop-fill-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-fill-rect (base) (stride) (px) (py) (pw) (ph) (color) =
    gop-rect-rows base stride px py pw ph color 0

  gop-rect-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-rect-rows (base) (stride) (px) (py) (pw) (ph) (color) (y) =
    if y >= ph then 0
    else let r = gop-rect-cols base stride px (py + y) pw color 0
    in gop-rect-rows base stride px py pw ph color (y + 1)

Section: Glyph

  gop-char-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-char-cols (base) (stride) (px) (py) (glyph) (fg) (scale) (col) =
    if col >= 8 then 0
    else let on = bit-and (bit-shru glyph (7 - col)) 1
    in let p = if on == 1 then gop-fill-rect base stride (px + col * scale) py scale scale fg else 0
    in gop-char-cols base stride px py glyph fg scale (col + 1)

  gop-char-rows : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-char-rows (base) (stride) (font) (px) (py) (cce) (fg) (scale) (row) =
    if row >= 16 then 0
    else let glyph = cbf-row font cce row
    in let r = gop-char-cols base stride px (py + row * scale) glyph fg scale 0
    in gop-char-rows base stride font px py cce fg scale (row + 1)

  gop-draw-text : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
 CLIPPED AT THE SCANLINE, and the clip is the whole point. The framebuffer
 is linear, so a glyph drawn past the end of a row does not fall off the
 screen: its pixels land on the NEXT row, on top of whatever is there. A
 line one character too long therefore destroys itself AND its neighbour,
 and it does it silently. Measured 2026-07-29 on two of the three sitting
 rungs at 1280x800: MscAlignProbe's LIVENESS line came back reading
 "ng)LIVENESS out-of-range" with its own tail sitting on its head, and
 KbdDiagProbe's instruction line the same way.

 Panel width is not ours to choose, and it is the one thing about the
 target machine we do not know before the sitting. A line that fits the dev
 box at 1280 wraps at 1024 and shreds the screen at 800, so without this
 bound every probe's legibility is a bet on the panel.

 Truncating at `stride` rather than at a visible width is deliberate: the
 padded region beyond the visible pixels is invisible but harmless, while
 the next scanline is not. Losing the tail of a long line is a failure the
 reader can SEE; overwriting the line above it is one they cannot.

  gop-draw-text (base) (stride) (font) (px) (py) (s) (fg) (scale) (i) =
    if i >= text-length s then 0
    else if px + (i + 1) * 8 * scale > stride then 0
    else let cce = char-code (char-at s i)
    in let c = gop-char-rows base stride font (px + i * 8 * scale) py cce fg scale 0
    in gop-draw-text base stride font px py s fg scale (i + 1)

Section: Scale

  ui-scale : Integer -> Integer
  ui-scale (w) = if w >= 1024 then 2 else 1
